  "body": "Browsers are currently implementing the customizable `<select>` element, which expands `<option>` elements to allow rich content.\n\nWhile the spec already disallows interactive content inside the <option> element, it currently provides no guidance on how user agents should handle violations of this rule. In current nightly builds across multiple engines, authors can insert interactive elements, including links and buttons, inside an `<option>`, and these elements remain at least partially functional.\n\nThis creates accessibility challenges because it's not clear how such content should be reachable via the keyboard or via AT.\n\nI think the specification should explicitly define the error-handling and rendering behavior when interactive content is encountered inside an `<option>`.\n\nPotential solutions might include:\n- Stripping the interactive elements during parsing, or ignoring the subtree.\n- Rendering the elements visually, but forcing them to be inert.",
